From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Claude Opus 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
Date: Thu, 13 Aug 2026 21:00:00 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] mmq: round the MoE J-tile cap to the nearest valid tile, not
 the next one up (+6.1% prefill, +3.3% ttft, decode-neutral)

0023 caps the `mul_mat_id` MMQ column tile near the expected expert segment
width: `target = 2 * ceil(ncols_dst / n_expert)`, then takes the first valid J
at or above it. The valid J grid on RDNA4 is coarse - 16, 32, 48, 64, ... - so
"first at or above" is only faithful to that 2x when the target happens to land
on a grid point. At the 512-token ubatch the cap was tuned against it does:
512*8/256 = 16, target 32, J = 32.

0062 changed the shape. The ranked 534-token prompt now arrives as ONE ubatch
instead of 512 + 22, so `cols_per_expert` is 17, target is 34, and the same
rule steps past 40 (not a valid config for Q4_K/Q5_K/Q6_K) all the way to
**J = 48** - 2.9x the mean segment rather than 2x. Nothing re-tuned the cap
when the ubatch it reads changed underneath it.

Rounding to the nearest valid J (ties to the smaller) restores J = 32 at 534
and is a no-op everywhere the target already lands on a grid point.
`GGML_CUDA_MMQ_MOE_J_FIRST_GE=1` restores the old rule and is the A/B toggle;
`GGML_CUDA_MMQ_MOE_J_TARGET=<n>` overrides the target for sweeping.

## Measured (R9700 gfx1201, 62-patch parent, same binary, env-toggled)

Kernel census at pp534 proves it fires and prices it - `mul_mat_q` over two
passes, J = 48 -> J = 32:

    mul_mat_q<Q4_K> 54.14 -> 49.25 ms      mul_mat_q<Q5_K> 35.97 -> 34.92 ms
    mul_mat_q<Q6_K>  4.10 ->  3.86 ms      total          100.79 -> 94.64 ms

llama-bench pp534, 5 interleaved rounds, alternating order, 5/5 separated:
new 4754.1 / 4747.9 / 4709.2 / 4705.5 / 4722.2, old 4585.0 / 4555.8 / 4542.5 /
4563.8 / 4539.7 -> +3.65% on the whole pass. tg128 160.09 vs 160.03, flat.

J is a sharp optimum at this shape, swept on the same binary at pp534:
J = 16 4318.7, **J = 32 4679.1**, J = 48 4506.8 (the shipped choice), J = 64
4399.3.

Ranked-harness replica (llama-server, 9 runs/arm, 6 interleaved arms/side),
medians, every arm separated 6/6 on both prefill and ttft:

    prefill  6723.4 -> 7132.9 tok/s   1.0609
    ttft     0.11639 -> 0.112675 s    1.0330
    decode   156.55 -> 156.47 tok/s   0.9995   (mmq is not on the decode path)

    score    0.9995^0.65 * 1.0609^0.20 * 1.0330^0.15 = 1.0165

## Correctness

The runner gate is `-c 512 --chunks 8`: one 512-token ubatch, target exactly
32, so both rules pick J = 32 and the arms are the same binary path. Measured,
3 loads each, zero spread: stock b10237 3.9314, candidate 3.9318 either way -
the series delta stays +0.010% against a 0.1% budget.

Both long windows are exact multiples of 512 and equally untouched: 16384 reads
6.0307 on both arms (2 loads each), 32768 reads 6.3312 on both.

Where it does fire it is not bit-identical - 0040 folds the routed weight
multiply and 0037/0038 the expert-slot reduction into the MMQ epilogue, so the
tiling also orders how expert contributions combine - and it moves TOWARD
stock. At `-c 534 -b 534 --chunks 8`, against stock's own single-ubatch reading
of that shape (b10237, `-ub 576`) at 3.2459: J = 32 reads 3.2521 (+0.19%),
the shipped J = 48 reads 3.2755 (+0.91%).
---
diff --git a/ggml/src/ggml-cuda/mmq.cuh b/ggml/src/ggml-cuda/mmq.cuh
--- a/ggml/src/ggml-cuda/mmq.cuh
+++ b/ggml/src/ggml-cuda/mmq.cuh
@@ -1585,20 +1585,62 @@
         getenv("GGML_CUDA_DISABLE_MMQ_MOE_J") == nullptr) {
         const int64_t cols_per_expert = (args.ncols_dst + args.nchannels_y - 1) / args.nchannels_y;
         int64_t target = 2*cols_per_expert;
+        {
+            const char * e = getenv("GGML_CUDA_MMQ_MOE_J_TARGET");
+            if (e != nullptr) {
+                target = atoi(e);
+            }
+        }
         if (target < 16) {
             target = 16;
         }
+        // The valid J grid is coarse (16, 32, 48, 64, ... on RDNA4), so
+        // "first valid J >= target" can land far above the target. At the
+        // 512-token ubatch this cap was tuned for, target is exactly 32 and
+        // the rounding is invisible; once 0062 lets a 534-token prompt through
+        // as one ubatch, target becomes 34 and the same rule jumps to J = 48 -
+        // 2.9x the mean expert segment instead of the 2x it means to express,
+        // and a measured 3.7% of the pass. Round to the NEAREST valid J
+        // instead (ties to the smaller), which restores J = 32 there.
+        //
+        // This is a tile decomposition, not a change of arithmetic per output
+        // element, but it is NOT bit-identical in this series: 0040 folds the
+        // routed weight multiply and 0037/0038 the expert-slot reduction into
+        // the MMQ epilogue, so the tiling also orders how expert contributions
+        // are combined. Measured at -c 534 -b 534 --chunks 8, the new choice
+        // moves TOWARD stock's own answer for that shape rather than away:
+        // stock (single 534 ubatch) 3.2459, J = 32 3.2521 (+0.19%),
+        // J = 48 3.2755 (+0.91%). The runner gate (-c 512 --chunks 8) and both
+        // long windows (16384, 32768) are exact multiples of 512, where target
+        // is 32 and both rules pick J = 32 - all three are unchanged to every
+        // digit, verified by kernel census and by perplexity on both arms.
+        const bool first_ge = getenv("GGML_CUDA_MMQ_MOE_J_FIRST_GE") != nullptr;
         if (target < J_best) {
+            int     pick = 0;
+            int64_t best_dist = INT64_MAX;
             for (int J = 8; J <= 128; J += 8) {
                 const ggml_cuda_mmq_config config = ggml_cuda_mmq_get_config(type, J, fallback, cc);
-                if (config.type == GGML_TYPE_COUNT || J < target) {
+                if (config.type == GGML_TYPE_COUNT) {
                     continue;
                 }
                 if (mmq_get_nbytes_shared(config, cc) > smpbo) {
                     continue;
                 }
-                J_best = J;
-                break;
+                if (first_ge) {
+                    if (J < target) {
+                        continue;
+                    }
+                    pick = J;
+                    break;
+                }
+                const int64_t dist = J >= target ? J - target : target - J;
+                if (dist < best_dist) {
+                    best_dist = dist;
+                    pick = J;
+                }
+            }
+            if (pick > 0 && pick < J_best) {
+                J_best = pick;
             }
         }
     }
-- 
